Your section on early game rangers is this:

--------
Rangers:
As the name implies, rangers are designed to kill monsters at range.
They start with a bow and arrows, and their quest artifact is a bow;
there's also a chance to fire an extra missile (of any sort--if you run
out of arrows, throw darts or stones). They also get a cloak of
displacement, which makes monsters attack where they think the ranger
is (rather than your actual location), unless they're elven, in which
case they get an elven cloak, which isn't as useful. Many players value
displacement over invisibility, as displacement works on all sighted
monsters, while invisibility doesn't work on monsters that can see
invisible. Unless you have infravision, it can be difficult to see
monsters approaching before they're in melee range; one good option is
to scare them (with a scroll or Elbereth), and shoot them in the back
while they're running away. Let the knights be honorable, you're here
to survive. Another issue is that arrows (and most other ranged
weapons) will tend to disappear after a while; blessing and enchanting
them will help cut your losses. Don't be afraid to keep some daggers,
which don't disappear when thrown, as backup weapons.
--------

You put a lot less value on elven cloak than I do.  MC=3 and
stealth.  Stealth isn't as nice as displacement, MC=3 is better
than MC=2, nearly a wash between the two types of cloak.

You also swap the relative priority of daggers and arrows
compared to my opinion.  Because daggers don't disappear
and arrows do, an early ranger does better advancing dagger
first and then bow/arrow in the mid-game.  When the player has
a reliable way of blessing a stack of arrows, that's the time to
start shooting them constantly.



